DOMAIN: Database Layer, ORM Models, and Migrations
FILES: src/db_monitor/core/db.py, src/db_monitor/extensions.py, app/models.py, src/db_monitor/migrations.py, app/migrate.py
ENTRY: engine and AsyncSessionLocal created at module import in extensions.py.

ENGINE: src/db_monitor/core/db.py → proxies to extensions.build_engine()
- create_async_engine(POSTGRES_URL, pool_size=DB_POOL_SIZE, max_overflow=DB_MAX_OVERFLOW, ...)
- Pool metrics instrumented via SQLAlchemy pool event hooks (connect/checkout/checkin/close/invalidate/reset)

SESSION FACTORY: src/db_monitor/core/db.py → proxies to extensions.AsyncSessionLocal
- sessionmaker(bind=engine, class_=AsyncSession, expire_on_commit=False)
- Usage: `async with AsyncSessionLocal() as session: async with session.begin(): ...`
- WARNING: AsyncSessionLocal is a sessionmaker, NOT a session instance

ORM MODELS (app/models.py, re-exported via src/db_monitor/core/models.py):
- Base — declarative_base()
- MonitoredTable — "monitored_tables" — {id, service_name, database_name, table_name, topic_name, is_active, created_at}
  - Unique index on (service_name, database_name, table_name)
- MonitoredColumn — "monitored_columns" — {id, table_id→FK, column_name, data_type, is_primary_key, is_nullable, audit_enabled}
  - Unique index on (table_id, column_name)
- KafkaEvent — "events" — {id, event_type, event_time, user_id, service_name, kafka_topic, kafka_partition, kafka_offset, source_table_id→FK, operation, row_identity JSONB, event_data JSONB, raw_payload, capture_time}
  - PARTITIONED BY RANGE (capture_time) — monthly partitions
  - Unique index: (kafka_topic, kafka_partition, kafka_offset, capture_time) — idempotent dedup
  - GIN index on row_identity
  - Composite cursor indexes for pagination
- KafkaEventLegacy — "kafka_events" — legacy raw value column, kept for migration backfill
- ColumnChange — "column_changes" — {id, event_id→FK, table_id→FK, column_id→FK, operation, row_identity, old_value, new_value, changed_at}
  - PARTITIONED BY RANGE (changed_at) — monthly partitions
  - GIN index on row_identity
- ConsumerCheckpoint — "consumer_checkpoints" — {id, consumer_group, broker_kind, broker_destination, broker_substream, broker_position, kafka_topic, kafka_partition, kafka_offset, last_event_time, updated_at}
  - Unique index on (consumer_group, broker_kind, broker_destination, broker_substream)
- DeadLetterEvent — "dead_letter_events" — {id, service_name, broker_kind, broker_destination, broker_substream, broker_position, kafka_topic, kafka_partition, kafka_offset, operation, raw_payload, error_message, failed_at, is_replayed, replayed_at, replay_error}
- ApiKey — "api_keys" — {id, key_hash, owner_name, role, is_active, expires_at, revoked_at, created_at}
- ApiAuditLog — "api_audit_logs" — {id, api_key_id→FK, endpoint, method, status_code, ip_address, timestamp}
- ApiAuditLogSpill — "api_audit_log_spill" — {id, entry JSONB, error_message, spilled_at, replayed, replayed_by, replayed_at}
- IngestionQuotaWindow — "ingestion_quota_windows" — {id, dimension, identity, window_start, event_count, updated_at}
  - Unique index on (dimension, identity, window_start)
- CustomerJWTSecretState — "customer_jwt_secret_state" — {customer_id PK, state JSONB, updated_at}
- CustomerLifecycleState — "customer_lifecycle_state" — {customer_id PK, state JSONB, updated_at}
- CustomerProvisionJob — "customer_provision_jobs" — {job_id PK, customer_id, state JSONB, updated_at}

MIGRATIONS (src/db_monitor/migrations.py):
- 14 tracked migrations (0001–0014), stored in schema_migrations table
- Advisory lock ID: 7273646 ("dbmonitor" on phone keypad) — prevents concurrent migration runs
- apply_migrations(session_factory, engine) → acquires lock, applies pending, releases
- validate_migrations(engine) → raises RuntimeError if any pending
- Migration 0013: converts events/column_changes to PARTITIONED BY RANGE
- Migration 0014: adds cursor pagination composite indexes
- create_monthly_partitions(connection, table, column, months_ahead=3)
- drop_old_partitions(connection, table, column, older_than) — drops child partitions, never DEFAULT

PITFALLS:
- Do NOT call Base.metadata.create_all() in production — use migrations
- Partition pruning requires WHERE clause on the partition key (capture_time / changed_at)
- ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING on events means duplicates are silently skipped
- Advisory lock is per-connection; only one migration process runs at a time

================================================================================
#ingestion #consumer #kafka #rabbitmq #broker #pipeline #batch #dlq #deadletter #checkpoint #offset #circuitbreaker
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Ingestion Pipeline & Consumer Service
FILES: src/db_monitor/ingestion/consumer.py (compatibility wrapper), app/consumer_service.py (MAIN consumer logic — ~1000 lines, the real implementation), src/db_monitor/ingestion/pipeline.py (compatibility wrapper), app/event_pipeline.py (filter/transform logic), src/db_monitor/ingestion/brokers.py (compatibility wrapper), app/message_brokers.py (Kafka/RabbitMQ adapter implementations), app/consumer/runtime_state.py (CircuitBreaker, consumer runtime dicts — ACTUAL implementation, not a shim), app/consumer/recovery.py (checkpoint persistence, DLQ record management — ACTUAL implementation), app/change_processor.py (ChangeProcessor — extracts column-level changes from events, supports batch and single-event processing)
NOTE: app/consumer/ contains real implementation files, NOT shims. The src/db_monitor/ingestion/ wrappers re-export from these.
ENTRY: consumer_task() in consumer_service.py — main async loop per broker cluster.

CONSUMER TASK SIGNATURE:
consumer_task(cluster_name, broker_kind, bootstrap_servers, consumer_group, topics, topic_partitions, connection_url, queue_names, prefetch_count, dlq_destination, enable_dlq, enable_batch, batch_size)

PROCESSING FLOW:
1. Build adapter via build_message_consumer(broker_kind, ...) — returns KafkaConsumerAdapter or RabbitMQConsumerAdapter
2. Build DLQ publisher via build_dead_letter_publisher(broker_kind, ...) — returns KafkaDeadLetterPublisher or RabbitMQDeadLetterPublisher
3. Connect with exponential backoff (MAX_CONNECTION_RETRIES=60, CONNECTION_RETRY_DELAY=5.0)
4. For each message: parse → filter → transform → quota check → schema discovery → insert → change extraction → broadcast → checkpoint → ack
5. Batch mode: accumulates batch_size events or 5s timeout, then bulk inserts

BATCH WRITING (INGESTION_BULK_WRITE_ENABLED=true):
- _store_batch_graph_with_retries(): single INSERT with ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING for entire batch
- Batch schema discovery: one query per unique (service, db, table) combo
- Batch row identity: one query for all PK columns across all table_ids
- Batch change extraction: one query for audit-enabled columns, bulk insert changes
- Falls back to per-event writes on batch failure

CONSUMER RUNTIME STATE (app/consumer/runtime_state.py):
- consumer_runtimes dict: per-cluster {running, connected, topics, lag_by_partition, lag_total, last_message_at, last_commit_at, ...}
- CircuitBreaker per cluster: can_execute(), record_success(), record_failure()
- consumer_task delegates to runtime state for health reporting

RECOVERY (app/consumer/recovery.py):
- persist_consumer_checkpoints(): upserts latest committed position per (consumer_group, broker_kind, destination, substream)
- _persist_dead_letter_event(): stores failed message in dead_letter_events table
- list_dead_letter_events(): returns DLQ records for operator inspection
- ConsumerStopRequested exception: raised to halt consumer on unrecoverable errors

DEAD LETTER QUEUE:
- DLQ flow: failed event → _persist_dead_letter_event() → optionally forward to broker DLQ topic
- DLQ replay: replay_dead_letter_event_record(dlq_event_id) reconstructs KafkaEvent from DLQ row, re-runs through normal ingestion
- Batch replay: replay_dead_letter_event_records(limit, include_replayed)
- Metrics: dlq_messages_total counter, dlq_records_pending gauge

CHANGE PROCESSOR (app/change_processor.py):
- ChangeProcessor class: extracts column-level changes from Debezium event payloads
- process_event(event, session) → single-event extraction, queries audit-enabled columns per table
- process_events_batch(events, session) → batch extraction: fetches columns once per table_id, computes deltas in memory, bulk inserts ColumnChange rows
- get_changes(table_id, column_id, row_identity, from_time, to_time, limit, offset, cursor_id, cursor_time, include_total) → paginated column change queries with keyset support (changed_at DESC, id DESC)
- get_value_at_time(service_name, table_name, column_name, timestamp) → point-in-time value lookup via latest ColumnChange before timestamp
- Operation mapping: INSERT → new values from 'after', UPDATE → diff before/after, DELETE → old values from 'before'
- Only extracts changes for columns with MonitoredColumn.audit_enabled=True

ADAPTERS (app/message_brokers.py):
- MessageConsumerAdapter protocol: start(), stop(), ack_batch(messages), __aiter__
- KafkaConsumerAdapter: aiokafka AIOKafkaConsumer, manual commit, lag tracking via highwater
- RabbitMQConsumerAdapter: aio_pika, prefetch_count, durable queues, async Queue bridge
- DeadLetterPublisher protocol: start(), stop(), publish_dead_letter(...)
- Factory: build_message_consumer(broker_kind, ...) and build_dead_letter_publisher(broker_kind, ...)

PITFALLS:
- Batch mode can fall back to per-event writes silently — check logs for "falling back to per-event"
- Circuit breaker skips messages when open; consumer stays connected but pauses processing
- ConsumerStopRequested is NOT an error — it's a controlled shutdown signal
- RabbitMQ adapter uses delivery_tag as offset equivalent; partition is always 0

================================================================================
#events #event #parser #parsing #payload #debezium #cdc #json #timestamp #operation #insert #update #delete
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Event Parsing & Source Metadata
FILES: src/db_monitor/event_parser.py, app/event_parser.py, app/source_metadata.py, app/row_identity.py, app/schema_discovery.py, app/repositories/events.py
ENTRY: parse_event_payload(raw_payload) → ParsedEvent dataclass.

PARSED EVENT (event_parser.py):
- ParsedEvent dataclass: event_type, event_time, user_id, service_name, event_data, raw_payload
- Best-effort extraction from JSON payload
- Event type priority: event_type → type → op → payload.op → "unknown"
- Event time priority: payload.ts_ms → event_time → ts_ms/timestamp_ms/ts → now()
- User ID priority: user_id → userId → user
- Service name priority: service_name → service → extract_source_service_name()

DEBEZIUM ENVELOPE HANDLING:
- extract_source_record(event_data) → returns source dict from Debezium envelope
  - Checks event_data.source first, then event_data.payload.source
- extract_source_coordinates(event_data) → SourceCoordinates dataclass
  - Normalizes: service_name (name|server), database_name (db|database|catalog), namespace_name (schema|schema_name), raw_table_name (table|collection)
  - SourceCoordinates.table_name → "{namespace}.{table}" or just "{table}"
  - SourceCoordinates.table_filter_names() → list of candidate names for config matching
- extract_operation(payload) → maps Debezium op codes: c→INSERT, r→INSERT (snapshot), u→UPDATE, d→DELETE

ROW IDENTITY (app/row_identity.py):
- extract_row_identity(event_data, primary_key_columns) → dict or None
- Preference: declared PK columns → "id" → *_id columns → any column containing "id"
- Returns {column: value} for non-null values only

SCHEMA DISCOVERY (app/schema_discovery.py):
- SchemaDiscovery class: processes events to discover/update table/column schemas
- SchemaCacheBackplane: Postgres LISTEN/NOTIFY for multi-instance cache invalidation
- process_event(event, session) → upserts MonitoredTable + MonitoredColumn, returns table_id
- process_events_batch(events, session) → bulk upsert, returns {id(event): table_id}
- Column extraction: tries Debezium schema envelope first, falls back to before/after payload inference
- _extract_columns_from_schema(): reads schema.fields → finds row container (after/before) → extracts field defs
- _extract_columns_from_payload(): infers columns from before/after dict keys + sample values
- Schema cache with TTL (default 60s) per (service_name, table_name)

EVENT PIPELINE (app/event_pipeline.py):
- EventPipelineConfig: loads FILTER_ALLOWED_EVENT_TYPES, FILTER_IGNORED_EVENT_TYPES, FILTER_ALLOWED_TABLES, FILTER_IGNORED_TABLES, VALIDATION_REQUIRED_FIELDS, TRANSFORM_MASK_FIELDS, CUSTOM_EVENT_PROCESSORS
- EventPipeline.should_process(event) → bool: type filtering → table filtering → required field validation
- EventPipeline.transform(event) → KafkaEvent: runs custom processors, applies field masking ("***MASKED***")
- Global singleton: event_pipeline = EventPipeline()

PITFALLS:
- Debezium payloads can be nested: event_data.payload.source vs event_data.source
- Schema envelopes may be absent — columns are then inferred from before/after payloads (best-effort)
- Table filter names include both raw and namespace-qualified forms for config matching
- Custom event processors are loaded via importlib from CUSTOM_EVENT_PROCESSORS env var

================================================================================
#repositories #repository #readmodel #query #pagination #keyset #auditlog #spill #replay #events #responsemodels
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Data Repositories, Query Patterns & Response Models
FILES: app/repositories/events.py, app/repositories/audit_log_replay.py, app/repositories/audit_log_spill.py, app/response_models.py, app/core/responses.py (re-export wrapper)
ENTRY: Instantiate with session factory, then call async methods.

EVENTS REPOSITORY (app/repositories/events.py):
- EventsRepository(session_factory): read-model for event query endpoints
- list_events(*, filters, limit, offset, cursor_id, cursor_time, include_total) → (events, total)
  - Keyset pagination: (capture_time DESC, id DESC) when both cursor_time and cursor_id provided
  - Fallback: offset pagination when cursor is absent
  - Cursor token format: base64-encoded {capture_time, event_id} (versioned v=1)
  - include_total=false skips expensive COUNT(*) — default for keyset pages
- event_stats() → {total_events, by_service, by_type, by_operation}
- EventQueryFilters dataclass: service_name, source_table_id, row_identity, event_type, start_time, end_time, search_term
- EventPageCursor dataclass: capture_time, event_id (composite keyset boundary)
- search_term uses raw_payload ILIKE — full-text search across raw payloads
- Cross-references: used by GET /events and GET /events/stats in routes/data.py (Block #api)

AUDIT LOG SPILL REPOSITORY (app/repositories/audit_log_spill.py):
- list_audit_log_spill_snapshot(limit=100) → list[dict]
  - Returns recent spill entries ordered by spilled_at DESC
  - Each dict: {id, entry (JSONB), error_message, spilled_at}
- Cross-references: used by GET /admin/audit-log/spill in routes/ops.py (Block #api)

AUDIT LOG REPLAY REPOSITORY (app/repositories/audit_log_replay.py):
- replay_spill_entries(limit=100, ids=None, actor=None) → {attempted, replayed, failed}
  - If ids provided: replay only those spill rows
  - Otherwise: replay up to limit most recent unreplayed rows
  - Marks each spill row: replayed=True, replayed_by=actor, replayed_at=now(UTC)
  - Inserts into api_audit_logs table (not raw engine — uses session for atomicity)
- Cross-references: used by POST /admin/audit-log/spill/replay in routes/ops.py (Block #api)

RESPONSE MODELS (app/response_models.py, Pydantic v2 BaseModel):
- TableListResponse — {tables: list[dict], count: int}
- TableColumnsResponse — {columns: list[dict]}
- EventItemResponse — single event row: {id, event_type, event_time, capture_time, user_id, service_name, operation, source_table_id, row_identity, event_data}
- EventsResponse — paginated events: {events, total, limit, offset, next_cursor}
- ChangesResponse — column changes: {service_name, table_name, row_identity, changes, count, total, limit, offset, next_cursor}
- PointInTimeValueResponse — point-in-time lookup: {service_name, table_name, column_name, timestamp, value}
- HealthResponse — health check: {status, shutting_down} + extra fields (ConfigDict extra="allow")
- ReadinessResponse — readiness check: {status, checks} + extra fields
- AppInfoResponse — app metadata: {title, description, healthy, shutting_down}
- AccessTokenExchangeResponse — token exchange: {access_token, token_type, expires_at, owner_name, role}
- WebSocketTokenExchangeResponse — WS token: {session_token, expires_at, owner_name, role}
- All re-exported via app/core/responses.py compatibility wrapper
- Cross-references: all used in routes/data.py, routes/auth.py, routes/ops.py (Block #api)

PITFALLS:
- EventsRepository.list_events() returns count=0 when keyset cursor is active (no COUNT query)
- replay_spill_entries() uses ORM session — if spill entry JSONB is malformed, the insert into api_audit_logs may fail silently (counted in 'failed')
- FakeEventsSession in tests returns count on first execute(), scalars on second — order matters
- Response models use ConfigDict(extra="allow") on Health/Readiness — dynamic fields are not validated
- EventQueryFilters.search_term does ILIKE on raw_payload — performance concern on large tables without GIN index on raw_payload

================================================================================
#api #routes #rest #http #endpoints #fastapi #tables #events #changes #pagination #cursor #health #readiness #admin #auth #token
================================================================================

DOMAIN: API Routes & HTTP Endpoints
FILES: src/db_monitor/api/router.py, src/db_monitor/routes/data.py, src/db_monitor/routes/auth.py, src/db_monitor/routes/ops.py, src/db_monitor/routes/utils.py, app/routes/
ENTRY: router = APIRouter() composed from data, ops, auth sub-routers.

ROUTE COMPOSITION (routes/__init__.py):
- router includes: routes_data.router, routes_ops.router, routes_auth.router

DATA ENDPOINTS (routes/data.py) — require viewer role:
- GET /tables → list all monitored tables
- GET /tables/{service_name}/{table_name} → table details + columns
- GET /tables/{service_name}/{table_name}/columns → column list
- GET /events → paginated events with filters (service_name, source_table_id, row_identity, event_type, start_time, end_time, search_term)
  - Supports keyset pagination: cursor={capture_time, event_id} or cursor_time+cursor_id
  - include_total=false skips expensive COUNT(*)
- GET /events/stats → aggregated event statistics
- GET /changes → paginated column changes (table_name, column_name, row_identity, from_time, to_time)
  - Keyset: cursor={changed_at, id}
- GET /changes/{service_name}/{table_name}/{column_name}/at?timestamp=... → point-in-time value lookup
- GET /changes/{table_name}/{column_name}/at → legacy-compatible route (resolves service.table notation)

AUTH ENDPOINTS (routes/auth.py):
- POST /auth/token → exchange credential for short-lived bearer token
- POST /auth/ws-token → exchange credential for WebSocket session token
- POST /auth/keys → create API key (admin only)
- GET /auth/keys → list API keys with status summary
- POST /auth/keys/{key_id}/rotate → rotate key, revoke previous
- POST /auth/keys/{key_id}/revoke → revoke key
- POST /auth/bootstrap → create first admin key (one-time, ALLOW_BOOTSTRAP must be true)
- POST /admin/customers/{customer_id}/auth/bootstrap → bootstrap customer scope
- GET /admin/customers/{customer_id}/auth/jwt → customer JWT state
- POST /admin/customers/{customer_id}/auth/jwt/rotate → rotate customer JWT secrets
- POST /admin/customers/{customer_id}/auth/jwt/recover → recover previous JWT secret

OPERATIONAL ENDPOINTS (routes/ops.py):
- GET /health → comprehensive health check (DB, consumer, lifecycle, audit log)
- GET /livez → liveness probe (process-level)
- GET /readyz → readiness probe (consumer + lifecycle + audit log health, 503 when not ready)
- GET /info → app metadata (requires viewer)
- GET /admin/checkpoints → consumer checkpoint state (admin)
- GET /admin/audit-log/spill → spilled audit log entries (admin)
- POST /admin/audit-log/spill/replay → replay spilled entries (admin)
- GET /admin/dlq → list DLQ records (admin)
- POST /admin/dlq/replay → batch replay DLQ (admin, requires X-DBM-Customer-ID)
- POST /admin/dlq/{dlq_event_id}/replay → single DLQ replay (admin, requires X-DBM-Customer-ID)
- GET /admin/customers/{customer_id}/lifecycle → customer lifecycle state (admin)
- POST /admin/customers/{customer_id}/suspend → suspend customer (admin, requires scope)
- POST /admin/customers/{customer_id}/resume → resume customer (admin, requires scope)
- POST /admin/customers/{customer_id}/upgrade → register upgrade target (admin, requires scope)
- POST /admin/customers/{customer_id}/provision-jobs → create provisioning job (admin, requires scope)
- GET /admin/customers/{customer_id}/provision-jobs → list jobs (admin)
- GET /admin/customers/{customer_id}/provision-jobs/{job_id} → get job (admin)
- POST /admin/customers/{customer_id}/provision-jobs/{job_id}/steps/{step_name} → update step (admin, requires scope)
- POST /admin/customers/{customer_id}/provision-jobs/{job_id}/execute → execute N steps (admin, requires scope)
- GET /admin/customers/{customer_id}/guardrails → namespace guardrails (admin)
- GET /admin/customers/{customer_id}/observability → observability labels + alert routing (admin)
- GET /admin/slo-policy → tenant cohort SLO policy (admin)

WEBSOCKET:
- /ws/events?session_token=... → real-time event stream (auth via session token, API key, or bearer)

METRICS:
- /metrics → Prometheus metrics endpoint (unauthenticated)

PAGINATION HELPERS (routes/utils.py):
- parse_timestamp(value, field_name) → datetime or None (ISO 8601)
- parse_json_object_query(value, field_name) → dict or None
- encode_cursor_token(timestamp, id_value) → base64-encoded opaque token
- decode_cursor_token(token) → (timestamp, id) or None
- timestamp_age_seconds(value) → float or None

PITFALLS:
- /changes table_name can be "service.table" or separate service_name + table_name params
- Cursor tokens are versioned (v=1); legacy JSON-object cursors still accepted
- Customer-scoped mutating ops require X-DBM-Customer-ID header matching path param
- /readyz returns 503 until consumer has committed at least once (last_commit_at not None)

================================================================================
#auth #rbac #apikeys #jwt #tokens #argon2 #hashing #bootstrap #admin #viewer #credentials #bearer
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Authentication, RBAC & API Key Lifecycle
FILES: src/db_monitor/auth.py, app/auth.py
ENTRY: authenticate_credentials() is the unified auth entry point.

AUTHENTICATION FLOW:
1. authenticate_credentials(api_key_header, authorization_header, session_token, expected_token_type)
   - Session token → _authenticate_bearer_token(token, expected_type)
   - Bearer header → _authenticate_bearer_token(token, "access")
   - API key header → get_current_api_key(header) — format "{id}.{raw_secret}"
2. API key lookup: split on ".", query ApiKey by id, verify argon2 hash
3. JWT decode: try JWT_SECRET first, then JWT_SECRET_NEXT (rotation support)

API KEY FORMAT: "{id}.{raw_secret}"
- Key hashed with argon2 before storage (first 72 chars of secret, legacy normalization)
- generate_new_api_key() → secrets.token_urlsafe(32)
- build_api_key_expiration(ttl_days) → datetime
- is_api_key_usable(record, now) → checks is_active, revoked_at, expires_at

JWT TOKENS:
- build_access_token(api_key_record) → (token, expires_at) — TTL: ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL_MINUTES
- build_ws_session_token(api_key_record) → (token, expires_at) — TTL: WS_SESSION_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS
- decode_session_token(token, expected_token_type) → claims dict or None
  - Claims: sub (key_id), role, owner_name, token_type, iat, exp
  - Tries JWT_SECRET, then JWT_SECRET_NEXT for rotation

RBAC DEPENDENCIES (FastAPI Depends):
- get_current_api_key(header) → ApiKey or None (raw API key)
- get_current_bearer_api_key(credentials) → ApiKey or None (bearer token)
- get_current_authenticated_api_key() → combines both
- require_valid_api_key() → raises 401 if no valid credential
- require_admin_role() → raises 403 if role != "admin"
- require_viewer_role() → raises 403 if role not in ("admin", "viewer")

CUSTOMER JWT SECRET MANAGEMENT (routes/auth.py):
- Per-customer active/next/previous secrets stored in customer_jwt_secret_state table
- Bootstrap: initializes active + next secrets, generation=1
- Rotate: promotes next→active, generates new next, increments generation
- Recover: restores previous_active as active (rollback)
- State persisted to DB and cached in-memory dict

PITFALLS:
- API key hash normalization: only first 72 chars of secret are hashed (legacy compat)
- Bootstrap endpoint is disabled in production (ALLOW_BOOTSTRAP=false)
- Cannot revoke the currently authenticated admin key (self-revoke protection)
- Customer JWT state is dual-written: in-memory cache + DB persistence

================================================================================
#websocket #ws #broadcast #realtime #live #backplane #listen #notify #pubsub #clustering
================================================================================

DOMAIN: WebSocket Broadcasting & Backplane
FILES: src/db_monitor/ws_manager.py, app/ws_manager.py
ENTRY: ws_manager = WebSocketManager() singleton.

WEBSOCKET MANAGER:
- connect(websocket, client_id) → accept + store in _connections dict
- disconnect(client_id) → remove from _connections
- broadcast(message, event_id) → fan out to all local connections + publish to backplane
- _broadcast_local(message) → JSON serialize, send to all connections, remove dead ones
- connection_count property → current connected clients

POSTGRES LISTEN/NOTIFY BACKPLANE:
- Enables multi-instance WebSocket fan-out without Redis
- Channel: WS_BACKPLANE_CHANNEL (default "db_monitor_ws_events")
- _publish_event(event_id) → pg_notify with {origin_instance_id, event_id}
- _run_backplane_listener() → asyncpg connection, add_listener, handles notifications
- _deliver_backplane_notification(payload) → loads KafkaEvent by id, broadcasts locally
- Ignores own notifications via origin_instance_id comparison
- Auto-reconnects on connection errors with 5s backoff

INSTANCE IDENTITY:
- Each WebSocketManager instance has a unique _instance_id (uuid4().hex)
- Used to filter echo: notifications from self are ignored

LIFECYCLE:
- start_backplane_listener() → creates background asyncio.Task
- stop_backplane_listener() → signals stop, cancels task, awaits completion
- Registered in managed_lifespan as non-critical task

PITFALLS:
- Backplane uses raw asyncpg connection (not SQLAlchemy) because LISTEN/NOTIFY requires persistent connection
- DSN is derived from POSTGRES_URL by replacing asyncpg driver prefix
- Dead connections are silently removed during broadcast (no error raised)
- Backplane is optional — disabled when WS_BACKPLANE_ENABLED=false

================================================================================
#metrics #prometheus #observability #counters #histograms #gauges #monitoring #tracing #otel
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Prometheus Metrics, Observability & Tracing
FILES: src/db_monitor/metrics.py, src/db_monitor/tracing.py, app/metrics.py, app/tracing.py
ENTRY: Import metric objects directly from db_monitor.metrics.

PROMETHEUS METRICS (metrics.py):
COUNTERS:
- db_monitor_events_consumed_total{service, operation}
- db_monitor_events_processed_total{service, operation}
- db_monitor_events_duplicate_skipped_total{service}
- db_monitor_events_failed_total{service, error_type}
- db_monitor_dlq_messages_total
- db_monitor_failed_auth_attempts_total{surface, credential_type}
- db_monitor_audit_log_records_dropped_total
- db_monitor_audit_log_flush_failures_total
- db_monitor_webhook_delivery_attempts_total{url, result}
- db_monitor_ingestion_quota_dropped_total{dimension, identity}
- db_monitor_ingestion_quota_throttled_total{dimension, identity}
- db_monitor_provisioning_step_transitions_total{customer_id, step, status}

HISTOGRAMS:
- db_monitor_db_write_duration_seconds{operation}
- db_monitor_kafka_commit_duration_seconds
- db_monitor_api_request_duration_seconds{endpoint, method}
- db_monitor_audit_log_flush_duration_seconds
- db_monitor_ingestion_quota_sleep_seconds
- db_monitor_provisioning_step_duration_seconds{customer_id, step, outcome}

GAUGES:
- db_monitor_active_connections, db_monitor_db_pool_size, db_monitor_db_pool_checked_in_connections, db_monitor_db_pool_overflow_connections, db_monitor_db_pool_utilization_ratio
- db_monitor_consumer_lag{cluster, topic, partition}
- db_monitor_consumer_current_offset{cluster, topic, partition}
- db_monitor_consumer_committed_offset{cluster, topic, partition}
- db_monitor_consumer_commit_lag{cluster, topic, partition}
- db_monitor_dlq_records_pending
- db_monitor_consumer_last_successful_commit_timestamp_seconds
- db_monitor_tables_discovered_total, db_monitor_columns_discovered_total
- db_monitor_circuit_breaker_state{breaker}
- db_monitor_webhook_circuit_breaker_state{url}
- db_monitor_audit_log_queue_size

AUDIT LOG (app/audit_log.py):
- AuditLogWriter: asyncio.Queue-based buffer, batched flush to api_audit_logs table
- enqueue(entry) → non-blocking put, drops on full queue with metric
- run() → background task: drain queue, batch flush with retry
- On persistent failure: spills to api_audit_log_spill table via raw engine
- Retry: 3 attempts with exponential backoff, then spill

OPEN TELEMETRY TRACING (tracing.py):
- Optional, enabled via OTEL_TRACING_ENABLED=true
- initialize_tracing(app, engine, service_name, exporter, otlp_endpoint, otlp_headers)
- start_span(name, attributes) → context manager for custom spans
- current_trace_context() → dict for structured log correlation
- Instruments FastAPI and SQLAlchemy automatically

PITFALLS:
- Metric labels are string literals — typo in label = new time series
- Audit log spill bypasses AsyncSessionLocal (uses raw engine) to survive DB outages
- Pool metrics are refreshed on every pool event (connect/checkout/checkin), not polled

================================================================================
#webhooks #http #outbound #hmac #signing #circuitbreaker #retry #delivery #notifications
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Outbound Webhook Delivery
FILES: src/db_monitor/webhooks.py, app/webhooks.py
ENTRY: webhook_notifier = WebhookNotifier(urls, timeout, shared_secret) singleton.

WEBHOOK NOTIFIER:
- send_message(payload) → list of failed delivery results
  - Fires all webhook URLs concurrently via asyncio.gather + to_thread
  - Each delivery: _deliver_with_retries(url, payload_body)
- _deliver_with_retries: checks circuit breaker, retries with exponential backoff
  - Max retries: WEBHOOK_MAX_RETRIES (default 3)
  - Backoff: WEBHOOK_RETRY_BACKOFF_SECONDS * 2^attempt
- _post_payload: urllib.request POST with JSON body
- _build_headers: Content-Type: application/json, User-Agent: db-monitor-webhook/1.0
  - If shared_secret: X-DB-Monitor-Signature: sha256={hmac_hex}

CIRCUIT BREAKER:
- Per-URL failure counting
- Opens after WEBHOOK_CIRCUIT_BREAKER_THRESHOLD consecutive failures (default 5)
- Recovery after WEBHOOK_CIRCUIT_BREAKER_RECOVERY_SECONDS (default 30s)
- State tracked in webhook_circuit_breaker_state gauge (0=closed, 1=open)
- When open: delivery skipped, metric incremented as "short_circuited"

PAYLOAD FORMAT:
- {"type": "new_event", "event": {id, event_type, event_time, user_id, service_name, operation, source_table_id, row_identity, event_data}}

PITFALLS:
- Webhook delivery runs in thread pool (urllib is synchronous) — blocks thread, not event loop
- Failed webhook deliveries are persisted as DLQ records with broker_kind="webhook"
- Circuit breaker is per-URL — one failing URL does not block others
- HMAC signature uses sha256 with shared secret as key

================================================================================
#retention #cleanup #partition #archive #deletion #lifecycle #maintenance
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Retention, Cleanup & Partition Maintenance
FILES: src/db_monitor/retention.py, app/retention.py
ENTRY: retention_cleanup_task() and partition_maintenance_task() — background asyncio tasks.

RETENTION POLICIES:
- events: KafkaEvent.capture_time, EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS
- column_changes: ColumnChange.changed_at, COLUMN_CHANGES_RETENTION_DAYS
- dead_letter_events: DeadLetterEvent.failed_at, DEAD_LETTER_RETENTION_DAYS
- api_audit_logs: ApiAuditLog.timestamp, API_AUDIT_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS

CLEANUP FLOW:
1. Check if table is partitioned (pg_partitioned_table)
2. If partitioned: drop_old_partitions() — DROP TABLE for child partitions with upper bound before cutoff
3. If not partitioned: batched DELETE with RETENTION_CLEANUP_BATCH_SIZE rows per batch
4. Optional: archive before delete (JSONL files in RETENTION_ARCHIVE_DIR)

PARTITION MAINTENANCE:
- partition_maintenance_task() runs once per day (86400s interval)
- create_monthly_partitions(connection, table, column, months_ahead=3)
- Creates monthly range partitions for events and column_changes
- Idempotent: skips existing partitions

CONFIGURATION:
- RETENTION_CLEANUP_ENABLED=false by default
- RETENTION_CLEANUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=3600
- RETENTION_CLEANUP_BATCH_SIZE=1000
- RETENTION_CLEANUP_MAX_BATCHES_PER_TABLE=10
- RETENTION_ARCHIVE_BEFORE_DELETE=false

PITFALLS:
- Partition DROP is instant but irreversible — no undo
- Default partition holds legacy rows that don't match any range
- Retention task is non-critical — failure does not trigger app shutdown
- Archive format is JSONL (one JSON object per line)

================================================================================
#provisioning #customer #tenant #onboarding #lifecycle #namespace #secrets #deploy #orchestration #controlplane
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Customer Provisioning & Control Plane
FILES: src/db_monitor/provisioning.py, src/db_monitor/provisioning_local.py, app/routes/ops.py (provisioning endpoints)
ENTRY: execute_provisioning_step(step_name, customer_id, job_id, note)

PROVISIONING STEPS (ordered):
1. register_customer
2. generate_namespace
3. provision_postgres
4. provision_broker_namespace
5. create_secrets_and_config
6. deploy_monitor_services
7. run_migrations_and_health_checks
8. bootstrap_and_activate

STEP EXECUTION:
- execute_provisioning_step() dispatches to registered step providers
- register_local_step_providers() registers local (in-process) adapters
- Each step returns {detail, observability_labels, alert_routing, guardrails}
- Step state machine: pending → in_progress → completed | failed
- Failed steps can be retried with retry_failed=true

CUSTOMER LIFECYCLE:
- States: active, suspended (tracked in customer_lifecycle_state table)
- Actions: suspend, resume, upgrade, bootstrap_activate
- State persisted to DB and cached in-memory dict

OBSERVABILITY & GUARDRAILS:
- build_observability_labels(customer_id) → Prometheus labels per customer
- build_alert_routing_config(customer_id) → alertmanager routing per customer
- build_namespace_guardrails(customer_id) → ResourceQuota, limits, NetworkPolicy templates

PITFALLS:
- Provisioning jobs are persisted in customer_provision_jobs table (JSONB state)
- Execution idempotency via execution_id query param
- Customer scope enforced by X-DBM-Customer-ID header matching path param
- Step providers are pluggable — local providers are in-process stubs

================================================================================
#quotas #rate #limiting #throttle #window #ingestion #source #tenant
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Ingestion Quota Enforcement
FILES: src/db_monitor/ingestion/quota.py, app/ingestion_quota.py
ENTRY: quota_limiter = IngestionQuotaLimiter(...) singleton, applied in consumer pipeline.

QUOTA LIMITER:
- apply(event) → QuotaDecision(allowed, dropped, throttled, sleep_seconds, dimension, key, limit)
- Two dimensions: source (service_name/topic) and tenant (tenant_id from payload)
- Per-identity overrides via INGESTION_SOURCE_QUOTAS / INGESTION_TENANT_QUOTAS JSON env vars
- Default limits via INGESTION_SOURCE_DEFAULT_EVENTS_PER_WINDOW / INGESTION_TENANT_DEFAULT_EVENTS_PER_WINDOW

WINDOW MECHANICS:
- Fixed time windows: INGESTION_QUOTA_WINDOW_SECONDS (default 60)
- Window start = floor(now / window_seconds) * window_seconds
- Persistent storage: ingestion_quota_windows table with UPSERT
- ON CONFLICT: increment event_count WHERE event_count < limit

MODES:
- "throttle" (default): sleep until window resets, capped at INGESTION_QUOTA_MAX_THROTTLE_SECONDS
- "drop": reject event immediately when limit exceeded

PITFALLS:
- Quota is per-replica unless using shared Postgres storage (default behavior)
- Throttle mode blocks the consumer coroutine — affects all events in the batch
- Tenant extraction is best-effort from payload (tenant_id, tenant, tenantId keys)
- Quota disabled by default (INGESTION_QUOTA_ENABLED=false)

================================================================================
#cli #commandline #terminal #tui #textual #dashboard #terminalui #screens #login #event_detail #admin #sdk #client #python #javascript #library #api
================================================================================

DOMAIN: CLI, TUI, Terminal Interface & SDKs
FILES: tui/app.py, tui/main.py, tui/client.py, tui/screens/dashboard.py, tui/screens/login.py, tui/screens/event_detail.py, tui/screens/admin.py, tui/presentation.py, sdk/python/, sdk/javascript/
ENTRY: tui/main.py — launches Textual app.

TUI ARCHITECTURE:
- Separate package: tui/pyproject.toml, tui/uv.lock
- Textual-based terminal UI with screens: Login, Dashboard, EventDetail, Admin
- tui/client.py: WebSocket client connecting to /ws/events endpoint
- tui/presentation.py: formatting helpers for terminal display

SCREENS:
- Login: API key input, token exchange
- Dashboard: live event stream, table summary, consumer health
- EventDetail: single event drilldown with column changes
- Admin: API key management, DLQ inspection, provisioning status

CLI ENTRY POINTS (from pyproject.toml):
- db-monitor → db_monitor.cli:main
- db-monitor-serve → db_monitor.cli:serve
- db-monitor-migrate → db_monitor.cli:migrate

PYTHON SDK:
- sdk/python/db_monitor_sdk/client.py: async HTTP client for DB Monitor API
- sdk/python/db_monitor_client.py: legacy single-file client
- Separate pyproject.toml for package distribution

JAVASCRIPT SDK:
- sdk/javascript/db_monitor_client.mjs: ESM module for Node.js/browser
- sdk/javascript_api_client.mjs: legacy API client

PITFALLS:
- TUI and SDKs are separate packages — must be installed independently
- TUI connects via WebSocket, requires valid session token
- TUI screen rendering depends on terminal size
- SDKs target the public REST API, not internal modules

================================================================================
#deploy #helm #terraform #kubernetes #k8s #azure #postgres #kafka #secrets #hpa #ingress #networkpolicy #pvc #servicemonitor
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Deployment Infrastructure (Helm Chart + Terraform)
FILES: deploy/helm/db-monitor/ (Chart.yaml, values.yaml, templates/), deploy/terraform/ (main.tf, variables.tf, outputs.tf)
ENTRY: helm install db-monitor ./deploy/helm/db-monitor -f values.yaml

HELM CHART (deploy/helm/db-monitor/):
- Chart: db-monitor v0.1.0, appVersion 0.1.0, type: application
- Templates: deployment, service, configmap, secret, hpa, pdb, networkpolicy, servicemonitor, pvc

HELM VALUES — KEY SECTIONS:
- replicaCount: 2 (default), autoscaling: min=2, max=10, CPU 70%, memory 80%
- image: ghcr.io/jobm/db-monitor, pullPolicy IfNotNotPresent
- securityContext: non-root (uid 1001), read-only rootfs, drop ALL capabilities
- podAntiAffinity: preferred during scheduling on different hosts
- podDisruptionBudget: minAvailable=1
- database: host/port/name/user/password or existingSecret, pool tuning (size=10, overflow=20, timeout=30s, recycle=1800s, prePing=true)
- broker.kind: kafka or rabbitmq
- kafka: broker, topics, consumerGroup, securityProtocol, TLS config
- rabbitmq: url, queues, prefetchCount, dlqQueue
- consumer: batchEnabled, batchSize=100, bulkWriteEnabled, dlqEnabled, readiness thresholds
- auth: jwtSecret, jwtSecretNext, existingSecret, algorithm, token TTLs
- ingestionQuota: enabled, windowSeconds, source/tenant quotas, mode, maxThrottleSeconds
- retention: cleanupEnabled=true, interval=3600s, batch=1000, maxBatches=10, archive settings, per-table retention days
- websocket: backplaneEnabled, backplaneChannel
- webhooks: urls, timeout, retries, circuit breaker settings
- tracing: enabled, exporter (console/otlp), endpoint, headers
- persistence: PVC for retention archive (10Gi default)
- monitoring.serviceMonitor: enabled, interval=30s, labels
- networkPolicy: enabled, extraEgress rules
- extraEnv, extraVolumes, extraVolumeMounts for extensibility

HELM DEPLOYMENT TEMPLATE:
- RollingUpdate: maxUnavailable=0, maxSurge=1 (zero-downtime deploys)
- Config checksum annotation triggers rollout on configmap change
- Liveness: /health, initialDelay=30s, period=15s
- Readiness: /readyz, initialDelay=10s, period=10s
- Startup: /health, initialDelay=5s, period=5s, failureThreshold=30 (150s max startup)
- envFrom: configMapRef + secretRef (all config injected as env vars)
- Volume: optional PVC for retention archive when archiveBeforeDelete=true

HELM SECRET TEMPLATE:
- stringData: JWT_SECRET, JWT_SECRET_NEXT, POSTGRES_URL (constructed from host/user/password), KAFKA_SSL_PASSWORD
- Secrets rendered inline when values provided; use existingSecret for production

TERRAFORM MODULE (deploy/terraform/):
- Provider: azurerm >= 3.0, Terraform >= 1.5
- Azure PostgreSQL Flexible Server: configurable SKU (GP_Standard_D2s_v3 default), version 15, storage 128GB
- HA: mode Disabled/SameZone/ZoneRedundant, standby zone
- Maintenance window: day_of_week + start_hour
- PostgreSQL config: wal_level=logical, max_connections=200, shared_preload_libraries=pg_stat_statements
- Firewall rules: map of CIDR ranges → allowed CIDRs
- Kafka topics: commented reference for self-managed Kafka (partitions, replication, retention)
- Key Vault: optional secrets for postgres URL and JWT secret
- Outputs: postgres_fqdn, postgres_connection_url (used by Helm chart values)

TERRAFORM VARIABLES — KEY GROUPS:
- create_postgres_server, postgres_server_name, resource_group_name, location
- postgres_admin_user/password, sku, version, storage_mb
- postgres_ha_mode, postgres_ha_standby_zone
- postgres_subnet_id, postgres_private_dns_zone_id (for VNet integration)
- postgres_allowed_cidrs (firewall rules)
- postgres_max_connections
- kafka_source_topics, kafka_topic_partitions/replication_factor/retention_ms
- create_key_vault_secrets, key_vault_id, jwt_secret
- tags (applied to all resources)

PITFALLS:
- Helm chart configures PostgreSQL URL from parts — password must be set or existingSecret used
- Terraform module does NOT provision Kafka — only comments reference kafka_topic resource
- Helm values.yaml defaults to production (app.env=production, schemaMode=validate)
- Secret template renders POSTGRES_URL with asyncpg driver prefix automatically
- HPA targets CPU 70% and memory 80% — ensure container resource limits are sized appropriately
- PDB minAvailable=1 means at least one pod must stay running during disruptions
- NetworkPolicy is disabled by default — enable for production with egress rules for broker + DB
- ServiceMonitor requires Prometheus Operator CRDs installed in cluster

================================================================================
#lifecycle #startup #shutdown #graceful #signals #tasks #background #readiness
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Application Lifecycle & Background Task Management
FILES: src/db_monitor/lifespan.py, src/db_monitor/lifecycle_manager.py, app/lifespan.py, app/lifecycle_manager.py
ENTRY: managed_lifespan() in lifespan.py — orchestrates startup/shutdown; lifecycle_manager singleton in lifecycle_manager.py.

MANAGED LIFESPAN (lifespan.py):
- managed_lifespan(app, lifecycle_manager, audit_log_writer, schema_cache_backplane, ws_manager, broker_clusters, consumer_task, retention_cleanup_task, partition_maintenance_task, ...)
- Registers signal handlers via lifecycle_manager.setup_signal_handlers(loop)
- Calls lifecycle_manager.startup() → init_db() + schema mode check
- Starts background tasks as asyncio.Tasks:
  - audit_log_writer.run() (critical=False)
  - schema_cache_backplane.start_listener() (critical=False)
  - ws_manager.start_backplane_listener() (critical=False)
  - consumer_task() per broker cluster (critical=True)
  - retention_cleanup_task() (critical=False, if enabled)
  - partition_maintenance_task() (critical=False, if partitioned tables exist)
- On yield: waits for shutdown signal
- On finally: lifecycle_manager.shutdown()

APPLICATION LIFECYCLE MANAGER (lifecycle_manager.py):
- ApplicationLifecycleManager wraps GracefulShutdownManager
- setup_signal_handlers(loop): SIGINT/SIGTERM → async shutdown task
- startup(): calls init_db() which runs migrations (apply or validate)
- shutdown(): delegates to GracefulShutdownManager
- is_healthy: startup_complete AND NOT shutting_down AND NOT task_failure

GRACEFUL SHUTDOWN MANAGER:
- register_task(task, critical=True): adds done_callback for failure detection
- Critical task failure → triggers controlled shutdown via create_task
- Non-critical task failure → logged but app continues
- shutdown(): cancels all tasks, waits with timeout (APP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS), cleans up DB
- shutdown_in_progress flag prevents double-shutdown
- Force exit on repeated signals (os._exit(1))

PITFALLS:
- Consumer tasks are CRITICAL — their failure shuts down the entire app
- Audit log, schema cache, websocket backplane are NON-CRITICAL — failures are logged but tolerated
- Shutdown timeout is configurable via APP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (default 45s)
- Signal handlers are Unix-only; Windows uses uvicorn defaults
- init_db() runs on startup — controlled by DB_SCHEMA_MODE (apply/validate/skip)

================================================================================
#conftest #fixtures #fakesession #mock #testing #pytest #isolation #monkeypatch #autouse
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Test Fixtures, Fake Sessions & Mock Patterns
FILES: tests/conftest.py, tests/test_routes.py (defines reusable fakes)
ENTRY: conftest.py configures sys.path and markers; test_routes.py defines the canonical fake session classes.

CONTEST.PY SETUP:
- Adds REPO_ROOT, APP_ROOT (app/), SRC_ROOT (src/) to sys.path at collection time
- pytest_collection_modifyitems: auto-marks all tests WITHOUT @pytest.mark.sandbox as @pytest.mark.core
- pytest_runtest_setup: skips @pytest.mark.sandbox tests when live stack unavailable (via examples.sandbox.preflight)
- No shared pytest fixtures defined — each test module defines its own fakes inline

FAKE SESSION CLASSES (defined in test_routes.py, pattern copied across test modules):
- FakeSession: minimal async session — __aenter__/__aexit__, execute() returns 1. Use for tests that don't inspect query results.
- FakeScalarResult(values): wraps a list, exposes .all().
- FakeExecuteResult(scalar_value, scalars): exposes .scalar(), .scalars().all(), .scalar_one_or_none(). Primary result wrapper.
- FakeEventsSession(events): pre-loaded events list, captures statements[], returns count on first execute(), scalars on second. Use for GET /events tests.
- FakeApiKeysSession(api_keys): pre-loaded API keys, captures statements[], returns scalars. Use for /auth/keys tests.
- FakeWriteSession: tracks .added[], .statements[], auto-assigns IDs on flush(). Routes execute() by table name in statement text (customer_jwt_secret_state, customer_lifecycle_state, customer_provision_jobs). Use for provisioning and JWT lifecycle tests.

AUTOMATIC FIXTURE (only shared fixture across tests):
- control_plane_session (autouse, in test_routes.py): monkeypatches routes_ops.AsyncSessionLocal to return FakeWriteSession(). Ensures all ops route tests use a fake DB session without explicit setup.

MOCKING PATTERNS:
- monkeypatch.setattr(module, 'AsyncSessionLocal', lambda: fake_session) — swap DB sessions
- monkeypatch.setattr(module, 'function_name', fake_function) — swap service functions
- SimpleNamespace(**kwargs) — quick mock objects for service dependencies
- monkeypatch.setenv('ENV_VAR', 'value') — swap config constants (read at import time)
- _make_event(event_data) helper — builds minimal KafkaEvent for pipeline tests

ISOLATION RULES:
- Tests use bare module imports (e.g., `import routes.auth as routes_auth`) because conftest adds app/ to sys.path
- Tests do NOT import from db_monitor.* — they test the legacy app/ modules directly
- Each test module is self-contained with its own fakes; no cross-test state leakage
- Provisioning/lifecycle tests clear module-level dicts (routes_ops.CUSTOMER_PROVISION_JOBS.clear()) in test body
- FakeWriteSession.customer_jwt_records etc. are in-memory stores that reset per test via fixture recreation

PITFALLS:
- FakeWriteSession.execute() routes by string-matching statement text — fragile if table names change
- control_plane_session is autouse in test_routes.py only — other test modules must patch independently
- Tests import from bare module names (config, models, auth) not from db_monitor.* — this works because conftest adds app/ to sys.path
- FakeEventsSession returns count then scalars on sequential execute() calls — order matters

================================================================================
#migration #sql #partitioning #schema #ddl #advisory #lock #backfill #version #tracked
================================================================================

DOMAIN: Database Migrations, Schema Evolution & Partitioning
FILES: src/db_monitor/migrations.py (Python-tracked migrations, 14 steps), migrations/sql/ (raw SQL supplements), app/migrate.py (CLI entrypoint)
ENTRY: apply_migrations(session_factory, engine) or validate_migrations(engine).

MIGRATION SYSTEM (migrations.py):
- 14 tracked Python migrations stored in MIGRATIONS tuple
- Each Migration(version, description, apply_callable) is idempotent
- Applied versions recorded in schema_migrations table
- Advisory lock ID: 7273646 — prevents concurrent migration runs via pg_try_advisory_lock
- Current schema version: 0014_cursor_pagination_indexes

MIGRATION LIST (in order):
0001_base_schema — Base.metadata.create_all() for fresh databases
0002_event_ingestion_columns — ALTER TABLE events ADD kafka_topic/partition/offset + unique index
0003_legacy_event_backfill — Migrate rows from kafka_events → events via parse_event_payload()
0004_api_key_lifecycle_columns — ALTER TABLE api_keys ADD expires_at/revoked_at + indexes
0005_recovery_tables — CREATE consumer_checkpoints + dead_letter_events tables
0006_bigint_kafka_offsets — ALTER COLUMN kafka_offset TYPE BIGINT (widens int32)
0007_row_identity_columns — ALTER TABLE events/column_changes ADD row_identity JSONB + GIN index
0008_broker_recovery_metadata — ALTER consumer_checkpoints/dead_letter_events ADD broker_kind/destination/substream/position
0009_customer_jwt_secret_state — CREATE customer_jwt_secret_state table
0010_customer_control_plane_state — CREATE customer_lifecycle_state + customer_provision_jobs tables
0011_ingestion_quota_windows — CREATE ingestion_quota_windows table
0012_api_audit_log_spill — CREATE api_audit_log_spill table (also in migrations/sql/0012_*.sql)
0013_event_partitioning — ALTER TABLE events/column_changes → PARTITION BY RANGE + monthly partitions
0014_cursor_pagination_indexes — CREATE composite indexes for keyset pagination on events/column_changes

RAW SQL FILES (migrations/sql/):
- 0012_api_audit_log_spill.sql — CREATE TABLE api_audit_log_spill + index (standalone SQL supplement)
- 0013_api_audit_log_spill_replay_columns.sql — ALTER TABLE ADD replayed/replayed_by/replayed_at + index
- NOTE: These SQL files are reference copies. The Python migrations.py is the authoritative migration runner.

PARTITIONING (Migration 0013):
- events table: PARTITION BY RANGE (capture_time), monthly partitions (e.g., events_202601)
- column_changes table: PARTITION BY RANGE (changed_at), monthly partitions (e.g., column_changes_202601)
- Both get a DEFAULT partition for legacy rows not matching any range
- Unique indexes recreated with partition key included (required by PostgreSQL)
- GIN indexes on row_identity preserved

PARTITION MANAGEMENT (retention.py + migrations.py):
- create_monthly_partitions(connection, table, column, months_ahead=3) — creates future monthly partitions
- drop_old_partitions(connection, table, column, older_than) — drops child partitions by upper bound, never DEFAULT
- partition_maintenance_task() — background task, runs daily, creates next 3 months of partitions
- retention_cleanup_task() — checks pg_partitioned_table, drops partitions if partitioned, else batched DELETE

ADVISORY LOCKING:
- Lock ID 7273646 constant shared across all replicas
- _acquire_migration_lock() → pg_try_advisory_lock (non-blocking)
- If lock held: raises RuntimeError with clear message
- _release_migration_lock() → pg_advisory_unlock in finally block
- Tests: test_migration_locking.py validates concurrent migration safety

CLI (app/migrate.py):
- db-monitor-migrate apply — runs all pending migrations with advisory lock
- db-monitor-migrate validate — checks pending without applying, raises if any
- Also accessible via: make monitor-migrate

SCHEMA MODE (DB_SCHEMA_MODE env var):
- apply: runs apply_migrations() on startup (development default)
- validate: runs validate_migrations() on startup, raises if pending (production default)
- skip: no schema changes on startup

PITFALLS:
- Migration 0003 backfill reads from kafka_events — if that table doesn't exist, ProgrammingError is caught and skipped
- Partition migration (0013) renames old tables to *_old, creates partitioned tables, migrates data, drops old tables
- Advisory lock is per-connection — only one process runs migrations at a time
- Raw SQL files in migrations/sql/ are NOT auto-applied — they are reference copies for documentation
- Partition key must be included in any UNIQUE index on partitioned tables
- DEFAULT partition catches rows that don't match any range — important for retention (never dropped)

================================================================================
#tracing #otel #opentelemetry #spans #otlp #distributed #correlation #traces #instrumentation
================================================================================

DOMAIN: OpenTelemetry Distributed Tracing Integration
FILES: src/db_monitor/tracing.py (canonical proxy), app/tracing.py (actual implementation), app/main.py (_configure_tracing)
ENTRY: initialize_tracing() called once at startup; start_span() used throughout.

TRACING INITIALIZATION (tracing.py — app/tracing.py):
- initialize_tracing(app, engine, service_name, exporter, otlp_endpoint, otlp_headers)
- Idempotent: global _instrumented flag prevents double-instrumentation
- Resource: Resource.create({"service.name": service_name})
- Provider: TracerProvider with BatchSpanProcessor for async batch export
- Exporter selection:
  - "console" → ConsoleSpanExporter (prints spans to stdout, for local debugging)
  - "otlp" → OTLPSpanExporter(endpoint=otlp_endpoint, headers=parse_otlp_headers(otlp_headers))
- Auto-instruments:
  - FastAPIInstrumentor.instrument_app(app) — HTTP request/response spans
  - SQLAlchemyInstrumentor().instrument(engine=sync_engine) — DB query spans
- OTLP_HEADERS format: comma-separated key=value pairs (e.g., "Authorization=Bearer xxx")

SPAN CREATION (start_span context manager):
- start_span(name, attributes) → context manager yielding span or None
- Best-effort: if opentelemetry not installed, yields None gracefully
- Tracer name: "db_monitor"
- Used in consumer_service.py for: kafka.consumer.message, kafka.process.event, kafka.process.batch, websocket.broadcast.event, websocket.backplane.deliver, dlq.replay.record
- Attributes follow OpenTelemetry semantic conventions: messaging.system, messaging.destination.name, messaging.kafka.partition, messaging.message.id, db_monitor.* custom namespace

TRACE CONTEXT LOGGING (current_trace_context):
- current_trace_context() → dict with trace_id (32-char hex) and span_id (16-char hex)
- Returns empty dict when no active span or opentelemetry not installed
- Injected into StructuredFormatter in app/main.py → every structured log includes trace_id and span_id
- Enables log-to-trace correlation in observability backends (Grafana, Jaeger, etc.)

CONFIGURATION (env vars):
- OTEL_TRACING_ENABLED=false (disabled by default)
- OTEL_SERVICE_NAME="db-monitor" (service identifier in traces)
- OTEL_EXPORTER="otlp" or "console"
- OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT (required when exporter=otlp, e.g., http://collector:4318)
- OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS (optional, comma-separated key=value)
- Startup validation: if otlp exporter selected without endpoint → config_error raised

STARTUP INTEGRATION (app/main.py):
- _configure_tracing(app) called after create_application()
- Checks OTEL_TRACING_ENABLED flag, skips if false
- Passes engine (SQLAlchemy async engine) for DB instrumentation
- StructuredFormatter always calls current_trace_context() for log correlation

SPAN INSTRUMENTATION POINTS (across codebase):
- consumer_service.py: "kafka.consumer.message" (per-message), "kafka.process.event" (per-event), "kafka.process.batch" (per-batch)
- ws_manager.py: "websocket.broadcast" (per-broadcast), "websocket.backplane.deliver" (per-backplane notification)
- consumer_service.py: "dlq.replay.record" (per-DLQ replay)

DEPENDENCIES (pyproject.toml):
- opentelemetry-api>=1.36.0 — trace API (no-op when disabled)
- opentelemetry-sdk>=1.36.0 — TracerProvider, processors, exporters
- opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-http>=1.36.0 — OTLP HTTP exporter
- opentelemetry-instrumentation-fastapi>=0.57b0 — FastAPI auto-instrumentation
- opentelemetry-instrumentation-sqlalchemy>=0.57b0 — SQLAlchemy auto-instrumentation

PITFALLS:
- Tracing is disabled by default — OTEL_TRACING_ENABLED must be explicitly set to true
- If opentelemetry packages not installed, start_span yields None (graceful degradation)
- SQLAlchemyInstrumentor instruments sync_engine only — async queries still get spans via the sync bridge
- BatchSpanProcessor buffers spans in memory before export — under high load, spans may be delayed
- OTLP_HEADERS must be comma-separated key=value, NOT JSON
- Console exporter is for debugging only — high overhead in production
- Tracer provider is set globally — only one initialize_tracing() call needed per process

